{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790255835,"nonce":"CsPW86JtlArn+DE9UcKUgfD30II=","title":"Lua: an absent field through a metatable is typed from the metatable itself, not its __index","message":"absent_field_ty passed each class shape to index_chain_ty as though the metatable were its own __index table. Right for the Class.__index = Class idiom; wrong for {__index = function} (typed nil instead of anything) and for a metatable without __index (typed from its own fields instead of nil). A read typed Int-or-nil that the __index function answers with a string then reinterprets the box: on main,\n\n  local function mk(v) return {x = v, y = 1} end\n  local t = mk(nil); mk(5)\n  debug.setmetatable(t, {__index = function(_, k) return 'debug ' .. k end})\n  print(t.x)\n\nprints a large integer instead of 'debug x'. The same program also shows that debug.setmetatable and debug.getmetatable never reach the typer: t's shape keeps no class, and a store through debug.getmetatable(o) into a class the types track is not seen. A lowering that trusts the types' account of a metatable chain (the closed field reads) then reads nil where the reference reads the __index answer.","files":null}]}